# The build context is the repository root.
#
# Deny everything, then name what the build actually reads. An allow-list rather than a
# deny-list for two reasons. `target/` is hundreds of megabytes on any machine that has
# run `cargo build`, and sending it would dominate the build time and invalidate the
# dependency cache on every source edit. And `config/peerConfig.toml` — the real one,
# with the TURN credentials in it — can never be baked into an image by accident,
# because nothing that is not listed below can enter the context at all. Only the
# example file is named, and it contains no credentials.
#
# **Podman reads this file; Docker does not.** Docker only looks for `.dockerignore`, and
# Podman falls back to that name only when this one is absent. That is not a problem for
# the deployment, which is Podman -- but it is worth knowing before running a build with
# something else and concluding from the result that the context filter works.
#
# It cost a CI failure once already. This file said `!docker` when the directory had been
# renamed to `containers`, and the podman build stopped with "no items matching glob ...
# (1 filtered out)". The local Docker build had passed, because after the rename Docker
# could no longer find an ignore file at all and therefore filtered nothing.

*

!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!src
!containers
!config/peerConfig.example.toml

# rust-toolchain.toml is intentionally absent: the Dockerfile pins the toolchain by
# image tag, and copying the file in would make rustup fetch components the container
# build does not use. See the comment in the builder stage.
